    titleEn: 'Desirable Difficulties: Why Effortful Friction Creates Mastery',
    subtitleEn: 'Robert and Elizabeth Bjork’s framework on why short-term struggle creates long-term durability.',
    shortDescEn: 'Learning conditions that introduce cognitive friction, effort, and temporary setbacks, which slow down initial acquisition but dramatically optimize long-term retention and transfer.',
    oneLineEn: 'If it feels easy and flowing, you aren’t learning; ease is the enemy of durability.',
    summary30sEn: 'Introduced in 1994 by UCLA cognitive psychologist Robert Bjork, the concept of "Desirable Difficulties" revolutionized pedagogical science. Bjork distinguished between *performance* (what is measurable right now during study) and *learning* (permanent neurological modification). Techniques that maximize rapid immediate performance (cramming, re-reading) lead to catastrophic rapid forgetting.',
    coreConceptEn: 'Memory storage operates under the "New Theory of Disuse": memories have both Retrieval Strength (accessibility in working memory) and Storage Strength (entrenched synaptic weight). When retrieval strength is high, studying does almost nothing to increase storage strength. Only when retrieval is difficult—forcing the brain to sweat and reconstruct traces—does storage strength multiply exponentially.',
    summary60sEn: 'Desirable difficulties include: spaced retrieval across days (spacing effect), mixing problem categories (interleaving), testing instead of studying (retrieval practice), varying the physical study environment, and self-generating answers. The key word is *desirable*: difficulties must be challenges the learner can successfully overcome, not bewildering confusion.',
    takeawaysEn: [
      'High immediate study performance is often an illusion that conceals zero long-term learning',
      'The harder it is to retrieve a memory, the more its permanent storage strength increases',
      'Spacing, interleaving, and self-testing are the core trio of desirable difficulties',
      'Welcome cognitive friction; smooth, effortless studying is a neurological dead end'
    ],
    whyItHappensEn: 'Synaptic consolidation requires biological stress: when retrieval is challenging, the hippocampus signals the cortex to allocate resources to reconstruct and reinforce pathways.',
    evolutionaryEn: 'Survival skills were acquired through life-and-death struggle and physical resistance; painless absorption was biologically impossible.',
    howItWorksEn: 'Memory allowed to partially decay -> Learner struggles to recall -> High cognitive effort expended -> Neural reconstructive circuits fire -> Storage strength surges -> Forgetting curve flattens.',
    visualHeadlineEn: 'Smooth Cramming vs. Desirable Difficulty Trajectory',
    visualDescEn: 'Robert Bjork’s performance vs. learning dichotomy.',
    sideALabelEn: 'Easy Cramming (Massed Review)',
